addDegrees -- changing the degrees of a toric vector bundle
***********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        F = addDegrees(E,L)
  * Inputs:
      * E, a *note vector bundle on a toric variety (Kaneyama's description):
        ToricVectorBundleKaneyama,
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, with matrices over *note ZZ:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
  * Outputs:
      * F, a *note vector bundle on a toric variety (Kaneyama's description):
        ToricVectorBundleKaneyama,

Description
===========


addDegrees replaces the degree matrices in E by the matrices in the *note List:
(Macaulay2Doc)List, L. The matrices in L must be $n$ by $k$ matrices over *note
ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, where $k$ is the rank of the vector bundle E and $n$ is
the dimension of the underlying toric variety. The list has to contain one
matrix for each maximal dimensional cone of the underlying fan over which E is
defined. Note that in E the top dimensional cones are already sorted and that
the degree matrices in L will be assigned to the cones in that order. To find
out the order use *note maxCones(ToricVectorBundle):
maxCones_lpToricVectorBundle_rp,. The matrices need not satisfy the regularity
condition. This can be checked with *note regCheck: regCheck,.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : E = toricVectorBundle(2,pp1ProductFan 2,"Type" => "Kaneyama")           |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = {dimension of the variety => 2 }                                        |
|      number of affine charts => 4                                           |
|      rank of the vector bundle => 2                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : ToricVectorBundleKaneyama                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : details E                                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = (HashTable{0 => (| 1 0 |, 0)  }, HashTable{(0, 1) => | 1 0 |})          |
|                      | 0 1 |                             | 0 1 |            |
|                1 => (| 1 0  |, 0)              (0, 2) => | 1 0 |            |
|                      | 0 -1 |                            | 0 1 |            |
|                2 => (| -1 0 |, 0)              (1, 3) => | 1 0 |            |
|                      | 0  1 |                            | 0 1 |            |
